WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:05.370
Film Courage: Do you think you have to be empathic or
open to be a good writer? Kelli McNeil, Author/Screenwriter/Producer: I mean isn't

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:13.410
that the job of being a writer is to
tell you know the story of humans? I mean

00:00:13.410 --> 00:00:17.850
you can certainly write a
blockbuster movie. I mean I know that

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:23.039
there there are a lot of big studio
platforms out there that just you know

00:00:23.039 --> 00:00:26.519
tell and I'm not going to name any
particular films but there are some that

00:00:26.519 --> 00:00:30.359
just don't have character they're all
action and maybe storylines but yeah I

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:36.870
think in order to to tell stories that
people gravitate to you you have to be

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:44.160
willing to tell you know a little bit of
yourself on each one one thing that

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.840
people always ask you when you are
writing is why are you the person to

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.699
tell this story
why why why should you be the voice of

00:00:53.699 --> 00:01:00.449
this and it's easier to I don't wanna
say prove but it's easier to persuade

00:01:00.449 --> 00:01:05.070
people that you are the right person to
tell a particular story if you yourself

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:08.340
can relate to it
I think that's what we're all looking

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:13.260
for you know any time that we go to a
movie we're looking to experience a kind

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:17.610
of catharsis you know we're looking to
experience some kind of human emotion

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:22.920
I find that my best pieces my best work
are the ones that I've really put myself

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:28.530
into and they have a bit of me in like
like the Baltimore school of charm

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:32.250
there's a reason that story is so
impactful there's a reason that it

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:37.110
resonates with people is because it's
it's inspired by my family it's it's

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:41.189
it's something that you know I've lived
through it's something that other women

00:01:41.189 --> 00:01:49.640
have lived through it's it's a bit of me
on that page absolutely you need to be

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:55.890
empathetic sure and at the same time
being empathetic and open also has its

00:01:55.890 --> 00:02:02.159
drawbacks and that may be things wound
do you more or or it's it's more intense

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:07.860
for you so how are you able to be open
and sensitive to things that are coming

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:13.090
at you for story ideas but then also be
able to receive notes

00:02:13.090 --> 00:02:16.900
and sometimes they're not always going
to be positive or go into a room and

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:20.050
maybe you read that room and it's not
friendly because I think that that's

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:23.950
learning the writing process you can
learn you know is it's like let's

00:02:23.950 --> 00:02:27.970
imagine we're building a building you've
got to have some kind of a floor plan to

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:30.370
do it right
you've got to know the rules before you

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:33.910
can break them so if you can learn
structural engineering and how to put it

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:37.660
together then you can shape it into
whatever shape you want it to be you

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:40.720
know you can you can make a skyscraper
with you know turrets on it if you want

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:44.920
it to but it that's how you that's how
you find your voice that's how you start

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:50.890
making stories your own learn the rules
before you break them so you know you

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:55.540
can't tell such an emotionally overdrawn
story you know for where you've got your

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:59.860
lead and crying or being assaulted or
abused for you know two and a half hours

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:04.390
nobody's gonna want to see that I think
that you as a writer need to understand

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:09.069
that you have a duty to your audience to
make sure you tell a story that moves

00:03:09.069 --> 00:03:13.569
forward and they tell a story that
progresses don't skimp on character

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:19.299
certainly save those those moments you
know of great emotion but learn how to

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:24.609
construct your story first you're
serving a lot of masters when you're

00:03:24.609 --> 00:03:30.730
when you're writing and if you don't
have good structure I mean unfortunately

00:03:30.730 --> 00:03:36.250
that's your story will fall apart how
does someone become good as a writer you

00:03:36.250 --> 00:03:41.370
said at first you weren't sure you could
do it but you wanted to definitely learn

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:45.310
have that as a safeguard you didn't want
to just try it and say you were a writer

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:49.359
without doing it you know I still have
problems saying I'm a writer I don't

00:03:49.359 --> 00:03:54.609
know when you ever become good I mean if
you're if you're Maya Angelou or

00:03:54.609 --> 00:03:59.340
somebody like that then you're
definitely a good writer but I think I

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:03.669
don't know I think I think asking
yourself the question always wanting to

00:04:03.669 --> 00:04:06.849
be better always knowing that you can
dig deeper and always finding that you

00:04:06.849 --> 00:04:14.620
can do something better that will help
you lead to improving your process and I

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:21.370
I I want to stay away from using labels
like good or great because that kind of

00:04:21.370 --> 00:04:25.380
puts you know that puts a lot of
pressure on yourself

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:28.530
especially for somebody that's just
starting out you have to be okay with

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:33.930
failing
you only need one story to spark the

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:39.750
next thing in your career I failed for
three years with with Baltimore and then

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:44.250
when I finally got it right it struck
the right chord what do you think the

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:50.000
difference was just working on it just
honing it I think I think it was my

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.610
dogged perseverance of not giving up on
it I I wanted people to see what I saw

00:04:56.610 --> 00:05:03.300
and I had to find a way to communicate
that out and I think just writing Draft

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:07.320
after Draft after Draft after draft and
I've probably gone through 30 iterations

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:12.090
of that script you know with time with
time I've certainly gotten better I've

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:17.310
improved my own process so I can shorten
that writing cycle but the first one I

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:22.590
had no idea what I was doing what were
some of the notes that you got and how

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:28.350
much of the feedback did you incorporate
to change I mean I incorporated feedback

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:31.740
